Hmm, my python replacement for print_literal is hacky and wrong — inline named args with Chinese… Actually simpler: `println!("{:<24} {:<28} {}", "ID", "摘要", "创建时间")` — clippy complains "创建时间" literal passed as format arg. Simplest fix: build the header as separate strings: `println!("{ID:<24} {summary:<28} {time}")` with variables. Let me just rewrite that line cleanly:

```rust
let (id_h, sum_h, at_h) = ("ID", "摘要", "创建时间");
println!("{id_h:<24} {sum_h:<28} {at_h}");
```

That's silly. Even simpler: drop the clippy pedantic print_literal by making it dynamic — the header row is genuinely dynamic-ish? No. Just do:

```rust
println!("{:<24} {:<28} {}", "ID", "摘要", "创建时间");
```
clippy::print_literal fires when args could be inlined into format string. Fix: inline: `println!("ID...")`? Chinese chars inline: `println!("{:<24} {:<28} 创建时间", "ID", "摘要")` — "创建时间" inlined as literal text, remaining args "ID", "摘要". That satisfies clippy. Let me do that.

