Two failures:

1. **Mojang manifest connection reset** — transient network issue (Connection reset by peer). The LlmClient has retry logic but HttpBase.get_json doesn't retry. Per NFR-3 (Network operations have timeout/retry), HttpBase should retry transient failures too. Add retry (2 attempts with backoff) to get_json/get_typed and download.

2. **Java supply failed: error decoding response body** — "error decoding response body" from reqwest means the JSON parse of the body failed... Actually `error decoding response body` is reqwest's error when .json() fails OR when the body decode (gzip) fails. In get_json I do resp.text() then serde parse → error would be Json variant. The `Http(reqwest::Error ... decoding response body)` comes from... resp.text()? That decodes the body per content-encoding. We enabled "gzip" feature on reqwest — it auto-decompresses when Content-Encoding: gzip. For Adoptium endpoint... Actually the error occurred in latest_jre's get_typed? The error chain says "Java supply failed: Network request failed: error decoding response body" — UpstreamError::Http(reqwest::Error) with kind Decode. resp.text() returning decode error — could be transient stream corruption or content-encoding mismatch.

Actually wait — maybe it's a timeout-ish truncation: 60+ seconds running then failed. The adoptium request may have been cut mid-body → decode error. This is a flaky-network issue; retry will fix both.

Let me add retry to HttpBase: wrap request execution with up to 3 attempts, backoff 1s/3s, on transient errors (connect/decode/timeout/5xx). Implement in get_json (and download start).

Also the earlier `Connection reset` on manifest — same retry fixes.

One concern: 120s total for the test run — the JRE download (~50MB from GitHub) may be slow here. Retry logic will help with transient failures.

Implement:

```rust
async fn get_with_retry(&self, url: &str) -> Result<reqwest::Response, UpstreamError> {
    let url = self.apply_mirrors(url);
    let mut attempt = 0;
    loop {
        attempt += 1;
        match self.http.get(&url).send().await {
            Ok(resp) if resp.status().is_success() || resp.status().as_u16() < 500 => return Ok(resp),
            Ok(resp) if attempt < 3 => { warn; sleep; continue; }
            Ok(resp) => return Ok(resp), // Non-2xx is handled by the caller (body needed)
            Err(e) if attempt < 3 && is_transient_reqwest(&e) => { warn; sleep; continue; }
            Err(e) => return Err(e.into()),
        }
    }
}
```

Hmm — cleaner: keep error semantics: return Ok(resp) regardless of status, let caller produce Status error, but retry 5xx first. Let me implement carefully in upstream.rs, used by get_json/get_typed/download.

is_transient for reqwest::Error: e.is_connect() || e.is_timeout() || e.is_decode() || e.is_request(). Decode retry is safe only if we haven't written anything — for get_json yes. For download, bytes_stream errors occur mid-write — retry would restart; we download to temp .part then rename, so restarting is safe (truncate temp). Add outer retry loop in download too: attempt download; on transient stream error → retry from scratch (temp file recreated).

Let me restructure download: 

```rust
pub async fn download(...) -> Result<PathBuf, UpstreamError> {
    let mut attempt = 0;
    loop {
        attempt += 1;
        match self.download_once(item, dest_dir, cancel.clone(), on_progress).await {
            Err(e) if attempt < 3 && is_transient_upstream(&e) && !cancel.is_cancelled() => { sleep; continue }
            other => return other,
        }
    }
}
```

And get_json: build status check into response handling with retries.

